---
// The pinned panel's head, shared by all eight case-study pages: the back link, the side
// marker, the title, and the ledger's first row — Type, which is the artifact type the
// index already printed for this page (`metaOf`, so the two can never drift).
//
// It exists because the alternative was eight copies of the same six lines, and the last
// time this site had a shape repeated by hand across eight pages the shapes drifted — the
// facts block was beat 2 on four pages and the last beat on three, which is the drift the
// page types were declared to stop (STYLE.md).
//
// The ledger is left OPEN: the component emits `.facts` and the Type row, and the page adds
// its own rows as children. So Type A follows with Role and Where, Type C with a count, and
// Type B adds nothing and still gets a ledger — which is why the Type B panels aren't a
// title floating over 300px of nothing.
import { metaOf } from '../data/experiments';
import SideMark from './SideMark.astro';
interface Props { href: string; title: string }
const { href, title } = Astro.props;
---
<div class="pin">
  <!-- ONE mark, doing both jobs (chooser: andrewshiau-panel-marks, Q5/06). This opened with a
       44px logo — a link home — and then the side marker, which draws the same figure again
       ~30px lower at a different size. Two drawings of one figure, stacked, in the first 60px of
       the panel: the reader's question is "what is this page", and the answer arrived twice in
       the same shape before the title.
       The marker is the one that carried a fact, so it took the link as well — see
       SideMark.astro, which now prints the WORDMARK next to the figure rather than the side
       word, because a link home that reads "Design + Build" is not findable. Its figure stays
       side-aware: the whole figure on the pages that sat on both halves of the practice, one
       object on the ones that didn't. The logo component that used to sit here is deleted
       site-wide (andrewshiau-mark-options, Q3/05), so these eight pages were simply the first to
       make the call. -->
  <SideMark href={href} />
  <h1>{title}</h1>

  <div class="facts">
    <div class="frow">
      <div class="fm">Type</div>
      <div class="ftx">{metaOf(href)}</div>
    </div>
    <slot />
  </div>

  <!-- NO CONTACT ROW (annotation: "dont think contact should be at top here remove from every
       page (execpt home obv)"). It was here on all eight case studies, in the panel, which put
       "email me" above the fold of a page a reader has not read yet — the ask arriving before
       the evidence. On the index that placement is right and stays: the panel there IS the
       introduction, and a hiring manager who has decided from the statement alone shouldn't
       have to scroll for the address. On a case study the reader is mid-argument.
       Nothing replaced it. The address is one click away on the index, and every case study's
       marker at the top of this panel is that click.
       `/gate/` keeps its own copy — see the note there; its whole content is "ask me for the
       password", so the address is the page's function rather than an appended CTA. -->
</div>

<!-- The split's rule. An empty element because it belongs to neither side; see
     `.splitrule` in global.css for why it isn't a border on the panel. -->
<div class="splitrule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
